            outputInterpretation: 'The Stommel model explains the fundamental asymmetry of ocean gyres:\n\n• Interior: broad, weak flow (paper example ~20 cm/s) driven by the wind-stress forcing term γ·sin(πy/b)\n• Western boundary region: the e^{Bx} term concentrates the flow into a narrow, intense jet (paper example: up to 240 cm/s northward, width < 100 km) — the Gulf Stream/Kuroshio\n• Without β (α = 0, f-plane): the gyre is symmetric and weak — the westward intensification is a direct consequence of the Earth\'s sphericity\n• WBC width δ = 1/α = R/(Dβ) decreases with: (1) smaller friction R, (2) larger β (stronger restoring force)\n• The Gulf Stream at Florida Strait: width ~100 km, speed ~2 m/s, transport ~30 Sv; at Cape Hatteras it separates from the coast and becomes unstable\n• The Munk model (1950) extended Stommel with lateral eddy viscosity A_H, giving δ_M = (A_H/β)^(1/3) ≈ 370 km for A_H = 1000 m²/s — wider because lateral mixing spreads momentum',
            accuracy: 'Solution algebra exact per Stommel 1948 eqs (9)/(19)–(22) — reproduces the paper\'s example quantitatively (α = 10⁻⁷ cm⁻¹, WBC < 100 km, max northward velocity 240 cm/s, interior ~20 cm/s, cgs inputs). ±25% typical for real-gyre application, dominated by the linear-friction idealization (R) and the missing lateral viscosity/inertia.',
            assumptions: ['Homogeneous (barotropic) ocean', 'Linear bottom friction −R·u, −R·v', 'Steady state; inertial terms omitted', 'Sinusoidal zonal wind stress F·cos(πy/b)', 'β-plane (f linear in y)'],
            limitations: ['Linear friction idealization (no lateral eddy viscosity — Munk model adds it)', 'No inertia, no topography', 'Single-gyre basin', 'Constants must use one consistent unit system (paper is cgs)']
