            outputInterpretation: 'The Kp index is the most widely used space weather severity metric:\n\n• Kp = 0–2 (quiet): no geomagnetic storm effects. Power grids operate normally. GPS accuracy is standard. Satellite anomalies are at baseline levels.\n• Kp = 3–4 (active): G0/G1. Aurora visible at high latitudes (Canada, Scandinavia). Weak HF radio absorption at high latitudes. Minor power grid fluctuations possible.\n• Kp = 5 (G1 storm): ~2000 days per solar cycle. Aurora visible from northern US (border states). Satellite orientation may need correction. HF radio degraded at high latitudes.\n• Kp = 6 (G2 storm): ~600 days per solar cycle. Power grid voltage alarms at high latitudes. High-frequency radio propagation degraded. GPS accuracy degraded at equatorial and auroral latitudes.\n• Kp = 7 (G3 storm): ~200 days per solar cycle. Aurora visible from mid-latitudes (New York, London). Satellite surface charging increases. Power grid transformers may experience damage. GPS positioning degraded significantly.\n• Kp = 8 (G4 storm): ~50 days per solar cycle. Aurora visible from Florida, Southern Europe. Widespread power grid voltage problems. Satellite anomalies common. GPS unavailable at high latitudes.\n• Kp = 9 (G5 extreme): ~4 days per solar cycle. Widespread power grid collapse possible. Satellite hardware damage. GPS totally unavailable for days. HF communications blackout.'
