            paperSummary: 'Shannon (1948) defined mutual information I(X;Y) = Σp(x,y)·log₂(p(x,y)/(p(x)·p(y))) as the reduction in uncertainty about X from knowing Y. In bits: I = H(X) − H(X|Y) = H(Y) − H(Y|X) = H(X) + H(Y) − H(X,Y). Mutual information is: (1) zero for independent variables (p(x,y)=p(x)·p(y) → I=0), (2) equals H(X) for perfectly correlated variables (Y fully determines X), (3) symmetric: I(X;Y)=I(Y;X). For Earth system digital twins, mutual information quantifies: (1) the value of an observation network — how much does assimilating observations reduce forecast uncertainty, (2) the complementarity of different satellite sensors — NDVI and LST may have overlapping information, (3) the optimal design of observation networks — where to place sensors for maximum information gain, (4) the dependence between Earth system variables — e.g., the mutual information between SST and precipitation identifies teleconnection patterns. MI is more general than linear correlation: it captures nonlinear and non-monotonic dependencies (e.g., the relationship between soil moisture and evapotranspiration is nonlinear, and MI captures it better than Pearson correlation).',
            scientificConcept: 'Mutual information measures the entropy reduction. For two Gaussian variables with correlation ρ: I = −½·log₂(1−ρ²). For ρ=0 (independent): I=0. For ρ=0.7: I=0.36 bits. For ρ=0.95: I=1.71 bits. For ρ=0.999: I=4.63 bits. The mutual information equals the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the joint distribution and the product of marginals: I(X;Y) = D_KL(p(x,y) || p(x)·p(y)). For continuous variables: I(X;Y) = ∫∫p(x,y)·log₂(p(x,y)/(p(x)·p(y)))·dx·dy. For a multivariate system (X = model state, Y = observations): I(X;Y) = ½·log₂(|P_f|/|P_a|) = ½·log₂(|B|/|P_a|) where P_f and P_a are forecast and analysis covariances. This provides a fundamental limit on the information extractable from observations: the "channel capacity" of the observation system.',
